<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umayyad Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">705–715</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Great Mosque of Damascus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Umayyad Mosque, or Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, was built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a> between 705 and 715 on the site of a Christian church of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>, which had replaced a Roman temple of Jupiter. Its vast courtyard, prayer hall and mosaics of landscapes and buildings, made with Byzantine craftsmen, set a model for later congregational mosques, and it still holds a shrine said to contain the head of John the Baptist.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque.jpg" alt="Umayyad Mosque" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umayyad_Mosque,_Damascus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
